---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import UnitsLifeRecipeScaler from '@/components/tools/units/UnitsLifeRecipeScaler.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="recipe-scaler">
  <UnitsLifeRecipeScaler client:load />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Paste the whole ingredient list</h2>
    <p>
      To scale a recipe by hand you do about fourteen small multiplications, and each one is a chance to
      drop a factor of two. The recipe scaler takes the whole ingredient list at once, finds the quantity at
      the start of each line, multiplies it, and rewrites the line with the rest of the text unchanged.
    </p>
    <p>
      Text that is not a quantity passes through, so headings, method notes and "salt to taste" stay, and
      the output is still a list you can cook from.
    </p>

    <h2>What the recipe scaler understands</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Input line</th><th scope="col">Doubled</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>2 cups all-purpose flour</td><td>4 cups all-purpose flour</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1 1/2 tsp baking powder</td><td>3 tsp baking powder</td></tr>
        <tr><td>3/4 cup granulated sugar</td><td>1 1/2 cups granulated sugar</td></tr>
        <tr><td>113 g butter, softened</td><td>226 g butter, softened</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2-3 tbsp water, as needed</td><td>4&ndash;6 tbsp water, as needed</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2 large eggs</td><td>4 large eggs</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Salt to taste</td><td>Salt to taste</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The parser reads fractions, mixed numbers, unicode fractions such as 1&frac12;, decimals and ranges.
      Results are rounded to fractions a measuring cup can hit: eighths for cups and teaspoons, quarters for
      tablespoons and ounces. You get 1 3/8 cups, never 1.3333 cups.
    </p>

    <h2>What scales linearly, and what does not</h2>
    <p>
      Ingredient quantities scale; cooking physics does not. After the tool does the arithmetic, adjust these
      by hand:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Salt and strong spices: perceived intensity is not linear with concentration. Scale to about
        85&ndash;90% and add more to taste.
      </li>
      <li>
        Raising agents: baking powder and soda scale roughly linearly up to a point, but a doubled batter in
        a deeper tin rises differently. Many bakers use about 90% when they double a recipe.
      </li>
      <li>
        Baking time and tin size: a doubled traybake in a bigger tin of the same depth needs about the same
        time. In the same tin it is twice as deep and needs much longer. Match the depth, not the volume.
      </li>
      <li>
        Anything you sear or fry: double the quantity in the same pan and it steams and does not brown. Cook
        in two batches; the total time more than doubles.
      </li>
      <li>
        Evaporation: a doubled sauce in a wider pan reduces at a different rate. Judge reductions by
        consistency, never by the clock.
      </li>
      <li>
        Alcohol and acid: wine, vinegar and citrus scale down better than up. Taste before you add the full
        amount.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Halving an egg</h2>
    <p>
      Eggs are the usual problem when you halve a recipe. Beat one egg fully, weigh it (a large egg is about
      50 g out of shell) and use half the weight. Half of an unbeaten egg gives an unpredictable mix of white
      and yolk, which matters in custards and enriched doughs.
    </p>

    <h2>Metric weights alongside</h2>
    <p>
      Turn on the metric option to add a gram figure to each cup and spoon measure. The figures come from
      the same ingredient density table as the grams-to-cups converter on this site. Known ingredients get a
      weight; others get a volume in millilitres.
    </p>

    <h2>Your recipe stays in the tab</h2>
    <p>
      The text you paste stays in this page. It is not saved to a server or sent with analytics, which
      matters if the recipe is unpublished or belongs to someone else.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
